WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.397 --> 00:00:03.792
- People should think, well,
"I feel fear about this,

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:06.137
"and therefore I shouldn't do it."

00:00:06.137 --> 00:00:08.127
It's normal to feel fear.

00:00:08.127 --> 00:00:10.108
Like, there'd have to
be definitely something

00:00:10.108 --> 00:00:11.771
mentally wrong if you didn't feel fear.

00:00:11.771 --> 00:00:13.546
And there's a friend
of mine who says, like,

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:15.502
"Starting a company is like staring

00:00:15.502 --> 00:00:18.335
"into the abyss and eating glass,"

00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:20.735
and there's some truth to that.

00:00:20.735 --> 00:00:23.737
And a successful company
is very much more about,

00:00:23.737 --> 00:00:26.014
"How quick are you to fix the mistakes?"

00:00:26.014 --> 00:00:27.581
Not, "Will you make mistakes?"

00:00:27.581 --> 00:00:29.481
The one thing I couldn't
compress was the cost of launch

00:00:29.481 --> 00:00:31.412
'cause there were only a few options,

00:00:31.412 --> 00:00:34.325
and the US options were way too expensive,

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:36.576
so I ended up going to Russia three times

00:00:36.576 --> 00:00:41.085
to try to buy the biggest ICBM
in the Russian nuclear fleet.

00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:44.016
- He's the CEO of SpaceX and Tesla Motors,

00:00:44.016 --> 00:00:46.010
and the chairman of Solar City.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.454
His goals of starting these businesses

00:00:47.454 --> 00:00:51.375
always revolve around changing
the world and humanity.

00:00:51.375 --> 00:00:55.129
He has an estimated net worth
of 11.5 billion dollars.

00:00:55.129 --> 00:00:56.962
He's Elon Musk, and here's my take on his

00:00:56.962 --> 00:00:59.965
top 10 rules to success, part two.

00:00:59.965 --> 00:01:01.384
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:02.794
and make sure to stick around all the way

00:01:02.794 --> 00:01:04.630
to the end for some special bonus clips.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.586
Also, as Elon's talking,
if he says something

00:01:06.586 --> 00:01:08.163
that really resonates with you,

00:01:08.163 --> 00:01:09.606
please leave it in the comments below.

00:01:09.606 --> 00:01:11.278
Put quotes around it so other people

00:01:11.278 --> 00:01:13.176
can be inspired as well.

00:01:13.176 --> 00:01:14.009
Enjoy.

00:01:17.055 --> 00:01:19.805
(dramatic music)

00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:30.047
- You are unusually
fearless and willing to go

00:01:30.047 --> 00:01:32.082
in the face of other people
telling you something is crazy,

00:01:32.082 --> 00:01:33.889
and I know a lot of pretty crazy people.

00:01:33.889 --> 00:01:35.250
You still stand out.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.958
Where does that come from,
or how do you think about

00:01:36.958 --> 00:01:39.116
making a decision when everyone tells you,

00:01:39.116 --> 00:01:40.419
"This is a crazy idea," or where do you

00:01:40.419 --> 00:01:43.257
get the internal strength to do that?

00:01:43.257 --> 00:01:45.010
- Well, first of all,
I'd say I actually think

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:49.093
I feel fear quite strongly,
so it's not as though

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:51.902
I just have the absence of fear.

00:01:51.902 --> 00:01:54.372
I feel it quite strongly.

00:01:54.372 --> 00:01:57.601
But there are just times when something is

00:01:57.601 --> 00:01:59.513
important enough, that
you believe in it enough,

00:01:59.513 --> 00:02:02.180
that you do it in spite of fear.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.322
- So, speaking of important things--

00:02:04.322 --> 00:02:06.905
- Like, people shouldn't think,

00:02:07.905 --> 00:02:09.415
"Well, I feel fear about this,

00:02:09.415 --> 00:02:11.836
"and therefore I shouldn't do it."

00:02:11.836 --> 00:02:14.021
It's normal to feel fear.

00:02:14.021 --> 00:02:15.789
Like, you'd have to
definitely have something

00:02:15.789 --> 00:02:19.765
mentally wrong with you
if you didn't feel fear.

00:02:19.765 --> 00:02:20.973
- So, you just feel it,
and let the importance

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:23.132
of it drive you to do it anyway?

00:02:23.132 --> 00:02:23.965
- Yeah.

00:02:23.965 --> 00:02:24.868
You know, actually, something that

00:02:24.868 --> 00:02:28.171
can be helpful is
fatalism, at some degree.

00:02:28.171 --> 00:02:31.329
If you just accept the probabilities,

00:02:31.329 --> 00:02:35.496
then that diminishes fear,
so when starting SpaceX,

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:41.382
I thought the odds of
success were less than 10%,

00:02:41.382 --> 00:02:44.176
and I just accepted
that actually, probably,

00:02:44.176 --> 00:02:46.593
I would just lose everything,

00:02:47.896 --> 00:02:49.897
but that maybe, we
would make some progress

00:02:49.897 --> 00:02:51.516
if we could just move the ball forward.

00:02:51.516 --> 00:02:54.487
Even if we died, maybe some other company

00:02:54.487 --> 00:02:58.391
could pick up the baton
and keep moving it forward,

00:02:58.391 --> 00:03:01.410
so that would still do some good.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:02.977
Yeah, same with Tesla.

00:03:02.977 --> 00:03:04.330
I thought, you know, odds of a car company

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.940
succeeding were extremely low.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.858
I would definitely advise people
who are starting a company

00:03:10.858 --> 00:03:14.941
to expect a long period
of quite high difficulty.

00:03:17.273 --> 00:03:18.106
- Yeah.

00:03:19.106 --> 00:03:23.109
- But, I mean, as long as
people stay super-focused

00:03:23.109 --> 00:03:25.871
on creating the absolute
best product to service

00:03:25.871 --> 00:03:28.797
that really delights their end customer.

00:03:28.797 --> 00:03:33.148
If they stay focused on
that, then basically,

00:03:33.148 --> 00:03:37.345
if you get it such that your
customers want you to succeed,

00:03:37.345 --> 00:03:39.044
then you probably will.

00:03:39.044 --> 00:03:39.877
- All right.

00:03:39.877 --> 00:03:41.697
You have to focus on the
customer and delivering for them.

00:03:41.697 --> 00:03:42.530
- Yeah.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:43.907
Make sure, if your customers love you,

00:03:43.907 --> 00:03:47.616
your odds of success of
are dramatically higher.

00:03:47.616 --> 00:03:50.253
One does have to be
focused on the short-term

00:03:50.253 --> 00:03:52.044
and money coming in
when creating a company,

00:03:52.044 --> 00:03:54.755
because otherwise, the company will die,

00:03:54.755 --> 00:03:58.998
so a lot of the times, people will think,

00:03:58.998 --> 00:04:02.295
like, creating a company
is going to be fun.

00:04:02.295 --> 00:04:03.939
I would say it's really not that fun.

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:05.779
I mean, there are periods of fun,

00:04:05.779 --> 00:04:09.663
and there are periods
where it's just awful.

00:04:09.663 --> 00:04:12.875
And particularly, if
you're CEO of the company,

00:04:12.875 --> 00:04:14.248
you actually have a distillation

00:04:14.248 --> 00:04:18.178
of all the worst problems in a company.

00:04:18.178 --> 00:04:19.506
See, there's no point
in spending your time

00:04:19.506 --> 00:04:21.603
on things that are going
right, so you're only

00:04:21.603 --> 00:04:24.857
spending your time on
things that are going wrong,

00:04:24.857 --> 00:04:26.152
and there are things that are going wrong

00:04:26.152 --> 00:04:28.634
that other people can't take care of,

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.228
so you have, like, the worst.

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:32.811
You have a filter for the
crappest problems in the company.

00:04:32.811 --> 00:04:33.775
(laughs)

00:04:33.775 --> 00:04:37.871
The most pernicious and painful problems.

00:04:37.871 --> 00:04:40.382
So I wouldn't say it's,
I think you have to feel

00:04:40.382 --> 00:04:45.095
quite compelled to do it
and have a fairly high

00:04:45.095 --> 00:04:47.070
pain threshold, and
there's a friend of mine

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.112
who says, like, "Starting
a company is like

00:04:49.112 --> 00:04:52.612
"staring into the abyss and eating glass."

00:04:53.519 --> 00:04:55.133
And there's some truth to that.

00:04:55.133 --> 00:04:57.606
(audience laughing)

00:04:57.606 --> 00:04:59.194
The staring into the abyss
part is that you're going to be

00:04:59.194 --> 00:05:03.970
constantly facing the
extermination of the company,

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.617
'cause most start-ups fail.

00:05:06.617 --> 00:05:09.367
Like, 99% of start-ups fail.

00:05:11.037 --> 00:05:14.854
So, that's the staring
into the abyss part.

00:05:14.854 --> 00:05:16.573
You're constantly saying, "Okay,

00:05:16.573 --> 00:05:20.740
"if I don't get this right,
the company will die."

00:05:21.626 --> 00:05:23.947
Should be quite stressful.

00:05:23.947 --> 00:05:26.864
And then, the eating glass part is,

00:05:28.141 --> 00:05:32.004
you've got to work on the
problems that the company

00:05:32.004 --> 00:05:35.310
needs you to work on, not the
problems you want to work on,

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:39.025
and so you end up working on problems

00:05:39.025 --> 00:05:42.396
that you'll really wish
you weren't working on,

00:05:42.396 --> 00:05:45.653
and so that's the eating glass part.

00:05:45.653 --> 00:05:47.371
And that goes on for a long time.

00:05:47.371 --> 00:05:48.965
- [Audience Member] So,
how do you keep your focus

00:05:48.965 --> 00:05:52.042
on the big picture when
you're constantly faced with,

00:05:52.042 --> 00:05:55.459
"We could be out of business in a month?"

00:05:56.357 --> 00:05:58.425
- Well, it's just a very small percentage

00:05:58.425 --> 00:06:01.381
of mental energy that's
on the big picture.

00:06:01.381 --> 00:06:04.710
Like, you know where you're
generally heading for,

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.034
and the actual path is
going to be some sort of

00:06:07.034 --> 00:06:09.867
zig-zaggy thing in that direction.

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:13.155
You're trying not to deviate
too far from the path

00:06:13.155 --> 00:06:15.731
that you want to be
on, but you're going to

00:06:15.731 --> 00:06:18.314
have to do that to some degree.

00:06:19.894 --> 00:06:21.531
But I don't want to diminish the,

00:06:21.531 --> 00:06:24.683
I mean, I think the profit
motive is a good one

00:06:24.683 --> 00:06:27.821
if the rules of an industry
are properly set up.

00:06:27.821 --> 00:06:29.356
So there's nothing
fundamentally wrong with profit.

00:06:29.356 --> 00:06:32.769
In fact, profit just means
that people are paying you more

00:06:32.769 --> 00:06:37.713
for whatever you're doing than
you're spending to create it.

00:06:37.713 --> 00:06:38.812
That's a good thing.

00:06:38.812 --> 00:06:39.645
(laughs)

00:06:39.645 --> 00:06:42.329
And if that's the not the case,

00:06:42.329 --> 00:06:45.997
then you'll be out of
business, and rightfully so.

00:06:45.997 --> 00:06:48.302
'Cause you're not adding enough value.

00:06:48.302 --> 00:06:50.216
First of all, I really need
to give some thought to, like,

00:06:50.216 --> 00:06:53.083
how can I provide advice
that would be most helpful,

00:06:53.083 --> 00:06:55.957
and I'm not sure I've given
enough thought to that

00:06:55.957 --> 00:06:57.863
to give you the best possible answer,

00:06:57.863 --> 00:07:02.030
but I think, certainly,
being focused on something

00:07:03.747 --> 00:07:08.173
that you're confident will have
high value to someone else,

00:07:08.173 --> 00:07:12.447
and just being really rigorous
in making that assessment,

00:07:12.447 --> 00:07:16.298
because people tend to,
a natural human tendency

00:07:16.298 --> 00:07:20.186
is wishful thinking, so a
challenge for entrepreneurs

00:07:20.186 --> 00:07:22.155
is to say, "Well, what's
the difference between

00:07:22.155 --> 00:07:26.591
"really believing in your
ideals and sticking to them

00:07:26.591 --> 00:07:29.549
"versus pursuing some unrealistic dream

00:07:29.549 --> 00:07:31.368
"that doesn't actually have merit?"

00:07:31.368 --> 00:07:34.881
And that is a really difficult thing to,

00:07:34.881 --> 00:07:36.943
can you tell the difference
between those two things.

00:07:36.943 --> 00:07:38.238
So, you need to be sort of

00:07:38.238 --> 00:07:41.238
very rigorous in your self-analysis.

00:07:43.099 --> 00:07:45.814
Certainly extremely tenacious,

00:07:45.814 --> 00:07:48.231
and then just work like hell.

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:51.887
I mean, you just have to put in, you know,

00:07:51.887 --> 00:07:54.554
80 to 100 hour weeks every week.

00:07:57.184 --> 00:07:59.670
- That is a lot of work.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:03.676
- All those things improve
the odds of success.

00:08:03.676 --> 00:08:06.887
If other people are putting
in 40 hour work weeks

00:08:06.887 --> 00:08:09.501
and you're putting in 100 hour work weeks,

00:08:09.501 --> 00:08:13.258
then even if you're doing the same thing,

00:08:13.258 --> 00:08:17.091
you know that in one
year, you will achieve in

00:08:19.128 --> 00:08:22.525
four months what it takes
them a year to achieve.

00:08:22.525 --> 00:08:24.640
- So failures are not
the most terrible things.

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:27.204
You have to learn from it and react to it.

00:08:27.204 --> 00:08:28.311
- Yeah, exactly.

00:08:28.311 --> 00:08:29.739
When you're building something new,

00:08:29.739 --> 00:08:32.916
there's going to be mistakes,
and it's important to

00:08:32.916 --> 00:08:35.861
recognize those mistakes,
acknowledge them,

00:08:35.861 --> 00:08:37.710
and take corrective action.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:40.927
And the success of a company
is very much more about

00:08:40.927 --> 00:08:43.085
"How quick are you to fix the mistakes?"

00:08:43.085 --> 00:08:44.769
Not, "Will you make mistakes?"

00:08:44.769 --> 00:08:46.121
- Or admit the mistakes.

00:08:46.121 --> 00:08:47.208
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:08:47.208 --> 00:08:49.328
And if you see the difference between

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:51.384
a start-up that is successful
and one that is not,

00:08:51.384 --> 00:08:53.371
it is because the successful one,

00:08:53.371 --> 00:08:54.788
they're both made of mistakes,

00:08:54.788 --> 00:08:57.294
but the successful one
recognized the mistakes,

00:08:57.294 --> 00:09:01.006
fixed them very quickly,
and the unsuccessful one

00:09:01.006 --> 00:09:02.860
tries to deny that the mistakes exist.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:04.436
- You know, extremely smart people

00:09:04.436 --> 00:09:07.100
are sometimes quite arrogant,
because they believe in

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.558
what they believe in, right,

00:09:08.558 --> 00:09:10.648
and so when they face criticism,

00:09:10.648 --> 00:09:14.828
it's less likely to admit
they can make mistakes.

00:09:14.828 --> 00:09:16.282
Was that in your case?

00:09:16.282 --> 00:09:20.546
- I learnt it when I was studying physics.

00:09:20.546 --> 00:09:23.592
In physics, you're taught
to always question yourself,

00:09:23.592 --> 00:09:25.809
you're taught to always
assume that you're wrong,

00:09:25.809 --> 00:09:27.800
not to assume that you're
right, and you have to prove

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:32.707
yourself not wrong, and so I
think that physics framework

00:09:32.707 --> 00:09:36.690
is really where I learnt
it, and it's very effective

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:40.852
for learning counter-intuitive
things that aren't obvious.

00:09:40.852 --> 00:09:41.787
- Mm.

00:09:41.787 --> 00:09:43.563
So, you are very famous
in saying that failure

00:09:43.563 --> 00:09:46.509
is actually an option,
and if you're not failing

00:09:46.509 --> 00:09:49.288
that means you're not innovative enough.

00:09:49.288 --> 00:09:50.121
- Yeah.

00:09:50.121 --> 00:09:50.954
I mean, it's not like I like failure.

00:09:50.954 --> 00:09:52.339
I mean, who likes failure?

00:09:52.339 --> 00:09:54.644
But if you only do things
that are certain to succeed,

00:09:54.644 --> 00:09:56.893
then you're only going to be
doing very obvious things.

00:09:56.893 --> 00:09:58.063
- A question that has been discussed

00:09:58.063 --> 00:09:59.820
over the past couple of days.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:03.987
Should we be considering
one-way only trips to Mars?

00:10:05.301 --> 00:10:09.820
What's the best approach
to colonize the planet?

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:12.368
Is it, well, what's your view?

00:10:12.368 --> 00:10:13.927
Is that socially acceptable?

00:10:13.927 --> 00:10:16.009
You think people will sign up to do it?

00:10:16.009 --> 00:10:16.842
- I think there's plenty of people

00:10:16.842 --> 00:10:19.324
that would sign up for
a one-way trip to Mars.

00:10:19.324 --> 00:10:21.242
(audience laughing)

00:10:21.242 --> 00:10:23.959
- Maybe if I could have a show of hands.

00:10:23.959 --> 00:10:26.425
Who would consider such an option?

00:10:26.425 --> 00:10:28.552
(audience chattering and laughing)

00:10:28.552 --> 00:10:29.385
I see some.

00:10:29.385 --> 00:10:31.592
Not many, but perhaps enough
for a couple of missions.

00:10:31.592 --> 00:10:33.116
(audience laughing)

00:10:33.116 --> 00:10:34.207
- Certainly be enough.

00:10:34.207 --> 00:10:35.436
I think it's sort of, like, is it a

00:10:35.436 --> 00:10:37.259
one-way mission and then you die,

00:10:37.259 --> 00:10:39.953
or is it a one-way mission
and you get resupplied?

00:10:39.953 --> 00:10:40.906
There's a big difference.

00:10:40.906 --> 00:10:42.989
(laughs)

00:10:44.177 --> 00:10:45.717
- We're for the second option.

00:10:45.717 --> 00:10:47.286
- Yeah, exactly.

00:10:47.286 --> 00:10:49.755
But I think it ends up being a moot point,

00:10:49.755 --> 00:10:52.485
because you want to
bring the spaceship back.

00:10:52.485 --> 00:10:54.949
Like, these spaceships
are expensive, okay?

00:10:54.949 --> 00:10:56.238
They're hard to build.

00:10:56.238 --> 00:10:58.905
You can't just leave them there.

00:10:59.783 --> 00:11:01.358
So whether or not people
want to come back or not

00:11:01.358 --> 00:11:03.440
is kind of, like, they
can jump on if they want,

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:04.812
but we need the spaceship back.

00:11:04.812 --> 00:11:08.863
(audience laughs and applauds)

00:11:08.863 --> 00:11:09.863
- Thank you.

00:11:11.996 --> 00:11:13.942
- I mean, it'd be kind of
weird if there was, like,

00:11:13.942 --> 00:11:17.093
this huge collection of
spaceships on Mars all the time,

00:11:17.093 --> 00:11:18.590
like, we would send them back.

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:20.111
I mean, of course we would send them back.

00:11:20.111 --> 00:11:23.511
- Now, with Tesla, your goal
has been to make a better car,

00:11:23.511 --> 00:11:26.154
and you've done that
with an electric vehicle

00:11:26.154 --> 00:11:30.498
that people covet, that
has quite a cult following,

00:11:30.498 --> 00:11:33.842
that's upgradeable, but
you also want to achieve,

00:11:33.842 --> 00:11:36.014
and your turn of phrase is very nice,

00:11:36.014 --> 00:11:40.706
or try to achieve this "platonic
ideal of a car," right?

00:11:40.706 --> 00:11:41.973
To reach for perfection.

00:11:41.973 --> 00:11:45.306
So, what does the perfect car look like?

00:11:46.768 --> 00:11:48.144
- Well, I mean, I do use that phrase

00:11:48.144 --> 00:11:50.122
with our engineering and science team,

00:11:50.122 --> 00:11:52.607
that aspirationally,
we're in pursuit of the

00:11:52.607 --> 00:11:56.061
platonic ideal of the perfect car,

00:11:56.061 --> 00:11:58.889
and who knows what that
looks like actually,

00:11:58.889 --> 00:12:03.579
but you want to try to make
every element of the car

00:12:03.579 --> 00:12:07.496
as flawless as possible,
and there'll always be

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:13.027
some degree of imperfection,
but try to minimize that

00:12:14.071 --> 00:12:18.002
and create a car that is
just delightful in every way,

00:12:18.002 --> 00:12:20.764
and I think if you do that, then the rest

00:12:20.764 --> 00:12:22.578
kind of takes care of itself.

00:12:22.578 --> 00:12:24.933
- You know, being innovative
can be a very exciting life,

00:12:24.933 --> 00:12:27.874
but doing business sometimes
requires, you know,

00:12:27.874 --> 00:12:31.821
persistence, and sometimes
could be very boring.

00:12:31.821 --> 00:12:34.479
Do you have the fun from innovation

00:12:34.479 --> 00:12:37.562
as from business, running a business?

00:12:39.219 --> 00:12:41.906
Or would you rather to
be just an innovator,

00:12:41.906 --> 00:12:44.768
engineer, instead of a
business owner or runner.

00:12:44.768 --> 00:12:47.073
- I mean, I'd love to just
do innovation and just do

00:12:47.073 --> 00:12:50.128
engineering, but you raise a good point,

00:12:50.128 --> 00:12:53.050
because, you know, a lot of
life, in general, any job,

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:55.096
there is, like, you
have to do your chores.

00:12:55.096 --> 00:12:56.554
You know?

00:12:56.554 --> 00:12:59.505
- 'Cause nobody else can do that for you.

00:12:59.505 --> 00:13:00.588
- Well, yeah.

00:13:00.588 --> 00:13:02.117
I think to be successful
at almost anything,

00:13:02.117 --> 00:13:04.617
you have to do the tough stuff

00:13:05.657 --> 00:13:08.024
as well as the enjoyable stuff.

00:13:08.024 --> 00:13:08.999
You have to do the boring stuff

00:13:08.999 --> 00:13:10.899
as well as the non-boring stuff.

00:13:10.899 --> 00:13:13.094
And if you don't do your chores,

00:13:13.094 --> 00:13:14.070
then bad things will happen.

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:16.202
But if they don't do the things
that they don't like to do,

00:13:16.202 --> 00:13:18.084
then the company will be in trouble.

00:13:18.084 --> 00:13:21.649
Like, you're basically, like,
it's more fun to cook the meal

00:13:21.649 --> 00:13:25.414
than to clean the dishes, but
you need to clean the dishes.

00:13:25.414 --> 00:13:26.247
(laughs)

00:13:26.247 --> 00:13:27.080
- You need to do both.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:27.913
- Yes, you need to do both.

00:13:27.913 --> 00:13:29.100
Exactly.

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.077
2001, I was just talking
to a friend of mine,

00:13:31.077 --> 00:13:34.043
and he asked me what I was
going to do after PayPal,

00:13:34.043 --> 00:13:37.318
and I thought, well, you
know, I was wondering,

00:13:37.318 --> 00:13:38.598
I'd like to get involved in space,

00:13:38.598 --> 00:13:40.326
but I just didn't think
there was anything I could do

00:13:40.326 --> 00:13:44.233
as an individual, but I
was curious as to when

00:13:44.233 --> 00:13:47.316
NASA would be sending a team to Mars,

00:13:48.778 --> 00:13:49.799
because that was always
going to be the thing to do

00:13:49.799 --> 00:13:54.044
after the moon, and I figured
that there'd be some plan

00:13:54.044 --> 00:13:57.951
and I'd just to the website,
and I could read the schedule.

00:13:57.951 --> 00:13:59.902
(laughing)

00:13:59.902 --> 00:14:03.531
Oh, yeah, it's like,
"Okay, 2017, good, okay."

00:14:03.531 --> 00:14:07.793
But actually, there wasn't
anything on the website,

00:14:07.793 --> 00:14:09.809
or at least, I thought,
like, can I not find it?

00:14:09.809 --> 00:14:11.996
Like, what's going on here?

00:14:11.996 --> 00:14:12.834
Is it secret?

00:14:12.834 --> 00:14:14.237
I don't know.

00:14:14.237 --> 00:14:17.727
But it turned out that
NASA had done a study

00:14:17.727 --> 00:14:21.972
on what it would cost to
do a man to Mars mission,

00:14:21.972 --> 00:14:25.494
and this was under Bush the first,

00:14:25.494 --> 00:14:29.082
and in his first year, he
asked for a 90-day study

00:14:29.082 --> 00:14:32.436
shortly after taking office,
and NASA came back with

00:14:32.436 --> 00:14:35.175
a 500 billion dollar
price tag, and he said,

00:14:35.175 --> 00:14:37.015
"Okay, maybe not."

00:14:37.015 --> 00:14:39.541
That's when 500 billion
dollars was serious money.

00:14:39.541 --> 00:14:41.729
(audience laughing)

00:14:41.729 --> 00:14:43.345
You know, for the government.

00:14:43.345 --> 00:14:46.178
So then, that got totally shelved,

00:14:47.891 --> 00:14:49.841
and it was, like, you are
not allowed to talk about

00:14:49.841 --> 00:14:53.591
any kind of crewed
mission to Mars at NASA,

00:14:53.591 --> 00:14:56.791
and anyways, so I thought,
"Well, if I could do something

00:14:56.791 --> 00:15:00.240
"that would galvanize public interest,

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:04.231
"and then that public
interest would translate to

00:15:04.231 --> 00:15:07.192
"additional appropriations for
NASA, increase their budget,

00:15:07.192 --> 00:15:09.864
"then maybe they could
do it," so actually,

00:15:09.864 --> 00:15:12.399
what I was sort of thinking I would do

00:15:12.399 --> 00:15:16.326
is send a small greenhouse
to the surface of Mars

00:15:16.326 --> 00:15:19.175
with seeds and dehydrated
gel, and then, upon landing,

00:15:19.175 --> 00:15:21.121
hydrate the gel, grow the plants,

00:15:21.121 --> 00:15:22.888
and the public tends to
respond to precedence

00:15:22.888 --> 00:15:24.494
and superlative, so this
would be the furthest

00:15:24.494 --> 00:15:26.830
that life's ever traveled,
the first life on Mars.

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:28.013
I was trying to figure out how to do this

00:15:28.013 --> 00:15:31.760
with the proceeds that I had from PayPal,

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:35.018
and I was able to figure out
how to get the cost of the

00:15:35.018 --> 00:15:38.640
spacecraft down and the
communications and the

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:40.833
little greenhouse and
everything, but the one thing

00:15:40.833 --> 00:15:42.794
I couldn't compress
was the cost to launch.

00:15:42.794 --> 00:15:44.480
There was only a few options,

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:47.898
and the US options were way too expensive,

00:15:47.898 --> 00:15:49.268
and so I ended up going
to Russia three times

00:15:49.268 --> 00:15:53.435
to try to buy the biggest ICBM
in the Russian nuclear fleet.

00:15:55.398 --> 00:15:56.438
- [Interviewer] That's
where I'd start, you know?

00:15:56.438 --> 00:15:57.918
Go big or go home, right?

00:15:57.918 --> 00:15:59.168
- I mean, okay.

00:16:03.038 --> 00:16:07.634
There were some strange
trips, that's for sure.

00:16:07.634 --> 00:16:10.862
But it was, like, virtually,
like, you can buy any,

00:16:10.862 --> 00:16:15.283
it's a very capitalist
society in some ways.

00:16:15.283 --> 00:16:17.319
So I actually did negotiate a deal to buy

00:16:17.319 --> 00:16:20.152
two of the ICBMs, minus the nukes.

00:16:22.229 --> 00:16:24.163
But I came to the conclusion
after that third trip

00:16:24.163 --> 00:16:27.004
that it wouldn't really matter.

00:16:27.004 --> 00:16:29.216
I actually came to the conclusion that my

00:16:29.216 --> 00:16:33.323
initial premise was wrong,
because I actually think

00:16:33.323 --> 00:16:36.402
there's a tremendous amount of will

00:16:36.402 --> 00:16:40.569
in the American population,
particularly to explore.

00:16:42.086 --> 00:16:45.500
United States, you know, maybe
more than any other country

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:49.740
is a distillation of the
human spirit of exploration,

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:51.646
and it's really fundamental to the psyche,

00:16:51.646 --> 00:16:53.870
so if people think there's a way,

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:55.937
I think you could actually
get a lot of support.

00:16:55.937 --> 00:16:59.217
But it can't just be banging
your head against a wall.

00:16:59.217 --> 00:17:00.861
You got to believe that this can be done

00:17:00.861 --> 00:17:03.662
without breaking the federal budget,

00:17:03.662 --> 00:17:06.912
so that's when I said, "Okay,
well, is there some way

00:17:06.912 --> 00:17:10.158
"to affect the cost of space transport,"

00:17:10.158 --> 00:17:13.825
and so I got together
with a group of people

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:18.774
over a series of Saturdays
just to try to say,

00:17:18.774 --> 00:17:21.654
"Is there something
fundamentally super-expensive

00:17:21.654 --> 00:17:25.821
"about rockets, or can the cost
be substantially improved?"

00:17:27.850 --> 00:17:31.894
And we had a bunch of those
kind of brainstorming sessions,

00:17:31.894 --> 00:17:34.826
and I couldn't see any
fundamental obstacle

00:17:34.826 --> 00:17:36.877
to improving the cost of the rockets,

00:17:36.877 --> 00:17:39.544
so that's when I started SpaceX.

00:17:41.675 --> 00:17:44.286
- Said, "I'll just build it myself."

00:17:44.286 --> 00:17:46.126
- But I'd say, definitely at that point,

00:17:46.126 --> 00:17:49.097
the probablity of success
was definitely less than 50%.

00:17:49.097 --> 00:17:51.014
I thought it would most
likely not succeed.

00:17:51.014 --> 00:17:52.793
But it was worth a try.

00:17:52.793 --> 00:17:55.096
- You're the CEO of SpaceX,
and you've said that

00:17:55.096 --> 00:17:59.128
your ultimate goal is to
get humankind to Mars.

00:17:59.128 --> 00:18:00.633
I've heard your response to the question,

00:18:00.633 --> 00:18:02.485
but these guys need to hear it.

00:18:02.485 --> 00:18:03.484
Why is Mars important?

00:18:03.484 --> 00:18:05.856
Why does Mars matter?

00:18:05.856 --> 00:18:08.609
- Sure, well, I think,
it's really a fundamental

00:18:08.609 --> 00:18:12.192
decision we need to
make as a civilization.

00:18:13.538 --> 00:18:15.288
What kind of future do we want?

00:18:15.288 --> 00:18:17.107
Do we want a future where
we are forever confined

00:18:17.107 --> 00:18:19.559
to one planet, until some
eventual extinction event,

00:18:19.559 --> 00:18:21.515
however far in the
future that might occur,

00:18:21.515 --> 00:18:24.870
or do we want to become
a multi-planet species

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:27.568
and then, ultimately, be
out there among the stars,

00:18:27.568 --> 00:18:30.671
and be among many planets,
many star systems?

00:18:30.671 --> 00:18:32.224
And I think the latter
is a far more exciting

00:18:32.224 --> 00:18:36.027
and inspiring future than the former.

00:18:36.027 --> 00:18:39.580
And Mars is the next natural step.

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:41.974
In fact, it's the only
planet we really have a shot

00:18:41.974 --> 00:18:45.474
at establishing a self-sustaining city on,

00:18:46.467 --> 00:18:49.591
and I think once we do
establish such a city,

00:18:49.591 --> 00:18:51.027
there will be a strong forcing function

00:18:51.027 --> 00:18:53.276
for the improvement of
space flight technology

00:18:53.276 --> 00:18:56.289
that will then enable
us to establish colonies

00:18:56.289 --> 00:18:58.081
elsewhere in the solar
system, and ultimately

00:18:58.081 --> 00:19:00.664
extend beyond our solar system.

00:19:02.314 --> 00:19:05.862
And so, there's the defensive
reason of protecting

00:19:05.862 --> 00:19:07.777
the future of humanity and
ensuring that the light

00:19:07.777 --> 00:19:09.762
of consciousness is not extinguished

00:19:09.762 --> 00:19:12.272
should some calamity befall Earth,

00:19:12.272 --> 00:19:14.692
but also, and that's the defensive reason,

00:19:14.692 --> 00:19:18.859
but personally, I find that
what gets me more excited

00:19:19.943 --> 00:19:23.814
is the fact that this would
be an incredible adventure.

00:19:23.814 --> 00:19:26.282
I mean, it'd be, like, the
greatest adventure ever.

00:19:26.282 --> 00:19:28.272
It would be exciting and inspiring,

00:19:28.272 --> 00:19:31.642
and there need to be things
that excite and inspire people.

00:19:31.642 --> 00:19:34.250
You know, after your reasons
why you get up in the morning,

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:36.914
it can't just be solving
problems, it's got to be,

00:19:36.914 --> 00:19:39.544
"Yeah, something great's going
to happen in the future."

00:19:39.544 --> 00:19:41.900
- [Host] Yeah, we talked about
this at length yesterday.

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:44.929
It's not an exit strategy or
a back-up plan for humankind

00:19:44.929 --> 00:19:49.096
when Earth fails, it's also
to inspire people on Earth,

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:54.730
and to transcend, to go
beyond our mental limits

00:19:57.039 --> 00:19:58.741
of what we think we can achieve.

00:19:58.741 --> 00:20:00.045
- All right, I mean, think of, sort of,

00:20:00.045 --> 00:20:01.892
how incredible the Apollo program was,

00:20:01.892 --> 00:20:05.367
and just, if you ask anyone and say,

00:20:05.367 --> 00:20:08.303
"Name some of humanity's
greatest achievements

00:20:08.303 --> 00:20:11.007
"of the 20th century," the Apollo program,

00:20:11.007 --> 00:20:15.174
landing on the moon, in many
places, would be number one.

00:20:18.604 --> 00:20:19.735
- Thank you guys so much for watching

00:20:19.735 --> 00:20:22.602
the part two series of
something that we're still

00:20:22.602 --> 00:20:24.585
experimenting with and
having some fun with,

00:20:24.585 --> 00:20:27.579
so let me know what you think,
if we should continue it,

00:20:27.579 --> 00:20:30.092
if there's someone else you want
to see a part two version of.

00:20:30.092 --> 00:20:31.740
Leave your thoughts in the comments below

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:33.450
and we'll see what we can do.

00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:35.049
Also, I'm really curious to figure out

00:20:35.049 --> 00:20:37.960
what did you learn most
from Elon in this video?

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:39.828
What message hit home
the hardest that you're

00:20:39.828 --> 00:20:43.395
going to immediately apply to
your life or business somehow?

00:20:43.395 --> 00:20:44.316
Leave it in the comments,

00:20:44.316 --> 00:20:45.948
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:20:45.948 --> 00:20:47.451
Finally, I want to give a quick shout-out

00:20:47.451 --> 00:20:48.728
to Kalie and Bradley.

00:20:48.728 --> 00:20:51.078
Thank you guys so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:20:51.078 --> 00:20:53.368
Your One Word, and taking that picture.

00:20:53.368 --> 00:20:55.951
It's awesome, and I really,
really appreciate it.

00:20:55.951 --> 00:20:57.553
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:20:57.553 --> 00:20:58.695
I believe in you,

00:20:58.695 --> 00:21:00.797
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:21:00.797 --> 00:21:02.768
and whatever your one word is.

00:21:02.768 --> 00:21:03.601
Much love.

00:21:04.458 --> 00:21:05.958
I'll see you soon.

00:21:11.788 --> 00:21:13.403
- But you have also said, in terms of

00:21:13.403 --> 00:21:16.060
where you want to go, that developing

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:18.925
a high-performance sports car
is not what this is about.

00:21:18.925 --> 00:21:20.090
- Absolutely, yes.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:21.628
- [Interviewer] This is
about something else,

00:21:21.628 --> 00:21:23.672
and you want to develop a sedan.

00:21:23.672 --> 00:21:26.237
- Yeah, the whole purpose behind Tesla,

00:21:26.237 --> 00:21:29.263
the reason I put so much
of my time and money

00:21:29.263 --> 00:21:32.947
into helping create the business is

00:21:32.947 --> 00:21:35.171
we want to serve as a
catalyst for accelerating

00:21:35.171 --> 00:21:36.979
the electric car revolution.

00:21:36.979 --> 00:21:39.720
The price of gas at the
pump does not reflect

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:41.766
the true cost of gasoline,

00:21:41.766 --> 00:21:45.087
because you have a
consumption of a public good.

00:21:45.087 --> 00:21:47.913
It's really a common problem in economics.

00:21:47.913 --> 00:21:49.726
You have the same thing in fishing,

00:21:49.726 --> 00:21:51.953
where because there's no
cost to fishing stocks,

00:21:51.953 --> 00:21:56.159
people just overfish and you
have disaster that ensues,

00:21:56.159 --> 00:21:58.317
and here, we're not paying for the cost

00:21:58.317 --> 00:22:00.635
of the CO2 concentrations in the oceans,

00:22:00.635 --> 00:22:03.247
the atmospheres, we're not
paying for all the auxiliary

00:22:03.247 --> 00:22:06.584
effects, the wars and the
other things at the gas pumps,

00:22:06.584 --> 00:22:08.328
so you effectively have
a subsidy taking place

00:22:08.328 --> 00:22:10.416
at the gas pump because of that.

00:22:10.416 --> 00:22:13.541
So the only way to bridge
that is with innovation,

00:22:13.541 --> 00:22:16.943
is to try to make electric cars better

00:22:16.943 --> 00:22:18.348
sooner than they would otherwise be.

